<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Framework config overrides for the bare-metal x86_64 PC target.

  Desktop windowing. AOSP ships every one of these off by default because the
  reference devices are phones; a PC with a keyboard, a mouse and a 2560x1600
  panel is the case they exist for. device/generic/goldfish/tablet does the same
  two, and this adds the ones that only make sense when the device really is a
  desktop.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Desktop mode itself. Without this the whole feature is inert no matter
         what else is set. -->
    <bool name="config_isDesktopModeSupported">true</bool>

    <!-- The built-in panel can host desktop sessions. On a phone this is false
         because the small screen only hosts desktops when an external monitor
         is attached; here the internal display IS the desktop. -->
    <bool name="config_canInternalDisplayHostDesktops">true</bool>

    <!-- Expose the developer-options toggle too, so desktop mode can be turned
         off at runtime without a rebuild while this is being brought up. -->
    <bool name="config_isDesktopModeDevOptionSupported">true</bool>

    <!-- Enter desktop mode by default rather than starting fullscreen and
         waiting to be asked. This is the difference between "a tablet that can
         do windows" and "a desktop". -->
    <bool name="config_enterDesktopByDefaultOnFreeformDisplay">true</bool>

    <!-- Show the home screen (and its wallpaper) behind floating windows, which
         is what makes it read as a desktop rather than a blank void. -->
    <bool name="config_showHomeBehindDesktop">true</bool>

    <!-- SQLite cursor window size, in KB. The platform default is 2048, and
         F-Droid crashes against it as soon as its index is downloaded:

           SQLiteBlobTooBigException: Row too big to fit into CursorWindow
               requiredPos=0, totalRows=8
             at org.fdroid.database.VersionDaoInt_Impl.getVersionsInternal
             at org.fdroid.database.DbUpdateChecker.getUpdatableApps

         A single row of F-Droid's Version table exceeds 2MB. The table carries
         whatsNew, antiFeatures, nativecode and features per version, and the
         localised changelogs of a widely translated app are enough on their
         own. Only eight rows were being read, so this is one oversized row
         rather than a large result set, and no paging on F-Droid's side would
         avoid it.

         2048 is a phone-era figure and this machine has 32GB of RAM, so the
         default is conservative by about four orders of magnitude here. 8192
         gives real headroom rather than moving the cliff slightly further away.
         The window is allocated per cursor and only for the duration of a
         query, so the cost is bounded by how many cursors are open at once.

         Not specific to F-Droid: any app reading a row bigger than 2MB hits
         this, and a desktop with this much memory has no reason to keep the
         phone default. -->
    <integer name="config_cursorWindowSize">8192</integer>

    <!-- Refresh-rate policy: sit at the panel's own top rate and never switch.
         GENERIC, no hardcoded Hz.

         On this AMD Phoenix laptop the 2560x1600 panel exposes its 120Hz and
         60Hz modes in DIFFERENT mode groups (dumpsys SurfaceFlinger:
         "primary (120 120)" is group 1/2, the 60Hz mode is group 3). A switch
         between groups is a full modeset that blanks the panel for a frame, and
         SurfaceFlinger's content-detection kept switching 120 to 60 for the
         mostly-static desktop, which looked like the desktop icons vanishing
         whenever the mouse moved and returning when it stopped.

         config_defaultRefreshRate=0 with a high config_defaultPeakRefreshRate
         means "default to the highest mode the panel actually has": 120 here,
         60 on a 60Hz-only panel, 144 on a 144Hz one, so nothing is hardcoded.
         Disabling content-detection switching (the sysprop in device.mk) then
         keeps it there instead of dropping to a lower, different-group mode.
         On a single-rate panel both are no-ops. -->
    <integer name="config_defaultRefreshRate">0</integer>
    <integer name="config_defaultPeakRefreshRate">240</integer>
</resources>
